{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Bindings key on the Jira KEY, which changes when an issue moves project\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nA Jira issue's KEY changes when it is moved between projects; its numeric `id` never does. Old keys\nare normally stacked in the `moved_issue_key` table so the old key still resolves — but a\nData-Center-specific Atlassian KB documents that third-party apps moving issues via workflow\npost-functions, automations or scheduled routines can FAIL to update that table, after which the old\nkey stops resolving entirely.\n\nrebar's bindings are keyed on the KEY, and pervasively so — not just `get_jira_key`/`get_local_id`,\nbut the `reverse` index, `note_absent`, `_retire`, `clear_absent` and `set_last_get` all take a\n`jira_key`. So a moved issue can silently detach: the binding points at a key that no longer resolves,\nthe absence probe reads it as gone, and the local ticket is orphaned from an issue that still exists.\n\n**What is NOT established, and must not be built on.** An earlier research pass asserted that\n`GET /rest/api/2/issue/{oldKey}` returns 200 with a body carrying the NEW key. That was retracted on a\nsecond pass and is unverified: the KB does not state whether a stale key yields 200-with-new-key, 301\nor 404. The remediation below is deliberately chosen to be correct under ALL THREE readings, so this\nstory does not block on settling it — but it is still settled here, as evidence, because two passes\ndisagreed and the next reader deserves the answer.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Record the numeric `id` alongside the key on every binding, and resolve by `id` when a key lookup\nmisses — re-keying the binding to the issue's current key when it does.**\n\nThis is deliberately ADDITIVE rather than a re-keying of the store, and that choice is the whole\ndesign decision:\n\n* the binding store is SHARED WITH CLOUD. Changing the store's identity from key to id would rewrite\n  the `reverse` index and every absence/retirement/last-get path for both vendors — a large blast\n  radius on the live Cloud bridge to fix a DC-specific detachment. Additive id-capture leaves every\n  existing key-keyed path behaving exactly as today.\n* it is correct under all three unresolved readings of what a stale key returns. If the old key still\n  resolves (200, with or without the new key in the body) nothing changes and the fallback never\n  fires. If it 404s, the id fallback recovers the binding. No detection heuristic depends on the\n  unverified behaviour.\n* it degrades safely on a store written before this change: an entry with no recorded `id` simply has\n  no fallback and behaves exactly as it does today. No migration is required, and the id is captured\n  opportunistically on the next successful read of that issue.\n\nConcretely: `bind_confirm` and `record_pending_key` accept and persist an optional `jira_id`; a\n`get_jira_id(local_id)` accessor is added; and the absence probe, before concluding an issue is gone,\nretries the lookup by recorded `id` and — on a hit whose key differs — updates the binding's key\n(maintaining the `reverse` index) and treats the issue as PRESENT rather than absent.\n\n**Rejected: re-keying the binding store on the numeric id.** It is the \"cleaner\" answer and it is what\nthe original ticket proposed, but it changes shared Cloud-facing identity semantics, rewrites the\nreverse index and every `jira_key`-taking method, and requires a store migration — all to reach the\nsame end state this achieves additively. If DC experience later shows the fallback firing routinely,\nre-keying becomes a justified follow-up with evidence behind it.\n\n**Rejected: detecting a move by parsing the old key's GET response.** That is precisely the mechanism\ntwo research passes disagreed about. Building on it would make the fix depend on unverified behaviour.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py` — persist and expose `jira_id`; re-key on an\n  id-resolved move.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — capture the numeric id\n  on reads (it is already present in the unwrapped REST payload as `id`).\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — held-out regression tests.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/` — the harness experiment recording what a stale key actually returns.\n\n## Testing\n\n* **the binding survives a move** — a fake client where the issue's key changes and the OLD key 404s,\n  while the recorded numeric id still resolves: assert the binding is preserved, re-keyed to the new\n  key, and NOT retired by the absence probe. This is the defect, so it is asserted end-to-end rather\n  than by inspecting the entry.\n* **the `reverse` index follows the re-key** — the old key no longer maps to the local id and the new\n  one does. A stale reverse entry would re-detach on the next pass.\n* **a pre-existing binding with no recorded id** behaves exactly as today (no fallback, no crash),\n  so the change is safe on an unmigrated store.\n* **the fallback does not fire when the key still resolves** — asserted by a client that fails the\n  test if the id lookup is attempted, since a fallback firing on the happy path would double every\n  absence probe's cost.\n* **live** — record what `GET /rest/api/2/issue/{oldKey}` returns on DC 8.17.1 after a real move,\n  as evidence, since two research passes disagreed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a binding whose issue moved project SURVIVES: with the old key 404ing and the recorded numeric\n      id resolving, the binding is preserved and re-keyed to the current key, asserted by a test that\n      FAILS against the current code\n- [ ] the absence probe does NOT retire an issue that is reachable by its recorded id — the specific\n      silent detachment this ticket exists to stop\n- [ ] the `reverse` index is updated in the same operation as the re-key, asserted, so the old key\n      stops resolving to the local id\n- [ ] bindings written BEFORE this change (no `jira_id`) continue to work unchanged, with no\n      migration step, asserted by a test over a legacy-shaped entry\n- [ ] the id fallback is NOT attempted while the key resolves normally, asserted by a client that\n      fails if it is called — the happy path must not pay for the recovery path\n- [ ] Cloud behaviour is unchanged: no existing `jira_key`-taking method changes signature or\n      semantics, verified by reading the diff, since this store is shared\n- [ ] [operator-attested] what `GET /rest/api/2/issue/{oldKey}` returns for a moved issue on DC 8.17.1\n      (status, and whether the body carries the new key) is RECORDED on this ticket as evidence — two\n      research passes disagreed and the answer must stop being folklore. The fix does not depend on\n      the answer; this criterion exists to settle it"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785521734064171001,"uuid":"87048941-2db3-4cda-a08e-ad8be273fb84"}